{"report": "At the previous release I called inflation down with conviction 0.65, based on a disinflationary trend in headline momentum after a peak near 2.81% y/y. The falsifier I set was two-fold: headline m/m would need to turn decisively positive (≥+0.25%) and sustain, or core PCE would need to stabilize or re-accelerate above 1.65%.

The current release shows mixed signals that partially falsify my prior conviction. On headline, the most recent m/m came in at +0.03%, failing the decisiveness threshold of +0.25%, so that trigger has not fired. However, on core PCE, the narrative is more ambiguous: the level sits at 1.58% y/y, which is above my stated 1.65% threshold only if read strictly as "stabilize above," but the 3-month core PCE annualized print of +1.69% is notably elevated. Core has stopped its outright decline—the 3-month yoy change is -0.10 pp, flat enough to suggest stabilization rather than continued deceleration.

Headline y/y has moved from 1.64% to 1.73%, a +0.09 pp increase that pushes back against the disinflationary signal. The 3-month annualized is barely positive at +0.08%, and headline momentum (the most recent m/m) remains modest at +0.03%. The range position metric shows we are only at 0.38 between the 12-month low and high, suggesting room to fall further—but the fact that headline has begun to drift upward in recent months contradicts the earlier downtrend.

Policy language remains consistent: inflation "running somewhat below 2 percent" and expected to "stabilize around" the 2% target medium term. This neutral framing does not flag acceleration risk.

I was right on the direction at the prior release (headline did continue lower to 1.64%), but I overestimated my conviction in a continued downtrend. The stabilization in core and the uptick in headline y/y suggest the disinflationary momentum has weakened. Headline CPI is no longer falling as reliably; it is edging upward. The next release is a close call between flat and down, leaning slightly toward flat given the mixed signals and fading momentum on the disinflation side.
